            <h1 class="mb-4">Classes of Our Submarines</h1>

            <p class="lead">Classes of ships are those that are built of essentially the same design. In the U.S. Navy, classes are named for the first ship or boat of the design ordered.</p>

            <p>Our very first motorized submarine was the USS Holland (SS-1), named after the designer and builder. This was purchased and commissioned in 1900. The first class of submarines would be the six boats we purchased of that same general design. Those submarines had names. However, that was changed in November of 1911 to a scheme where the classes were designated sequentially by letter. Thus, the first group of six boats became the &ldquo;A&rdquo; class. Boats within the class were numbered sequentially.</p>

            <p>For example, the early submarine Pike was renamed the A-5, the fifth boat in the first class. In a later example, the submarine Skate was renamed F-4, the fourth boat in the F, or sixth, class of submarines that we built.</p>

            <p>We had many classes of submarines prior to World War 1 as development progressed quickly. By the time of World War 1, we were building the R and S classes. After WW1, we found that we had a lot of work to do to catch up to the German designs. The R and S boats were already outdated. We did some major redesigns, and again built smaller classes of boats to prove new concepts. Each class was now being designated by the name of the first boat of the design ordered.</p>

            <p>At this point, we had returned to naming our submarines after sea creatures. Not all were fish. For example, some were named after whales and other aquatic mammals such as porpoises, seals and sealions. However, we did not go back and give the older boats names. They were still known as O, R and S boats.</p>

            <p>Most of the older O, R, and S classes were 200-foot boats, meaning they were designed for a maximum of 300-foot depths and were tested to 200 feet.</p>

            <p>Refer to Appendix M for comparisons of the classes of our lost submarines.</p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4 mb-3">Pre-War Classes Used in WW2</h2>

            <p id="o-class"><strong>O class</strong> &ndash; Built during WW1. These were mostly used as trainers in WW2. One was lost in 1923 and another in 1941 when preparing it for use as a trainer.</p>

            <p id="r-class"><strong>R class</strong> &ndash; Also built during WW1. These were also mostly used as trainers in WW2 and one was lost during the war while training.</p>

            <p id="s-class"><strong>S class</strong> &ndash; This was actually a series of classes built late in WW1 and shortly thereafter. Some of these boats served in the early months of WW2 until we could build enough new boats to replace them. Then many became training boats. S boats were significantly smaller than the Balao class, such as the USS Pampanito, and had a shorter range.</p>

            <p id="argonaut"><strong>Argonaut</strong> &ndash; This, and the Narwhal class, were larger, slower boats and had two six-inch guns. These subs were often used as minelayers or transports.</p>

            <p id="porpoise-class">The Porpoise, Perch, Salmon, Sargo and Tambor classes, which were similar, were built in the mid to late 1930s. They were larger than the S boats and only a bit smaller than the USS Pampanito. They were 250-foot depth boats and had a similar range to the Gato/Balao/Tench boats. These earlier classes may have carried 20 torpedoes rather than 24. The first fully welded pressure hull was a Porpoise class boat. (Prior designs were riveted.) The Porpoise, Perch, Salmon, Sargo and Tambor classes were our front-line boats at the beginning of the war. Because of the length of time they served in combat, these classes suffered the highest rates of loss.</p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4 mb-3">Classes Built For and During WW2</h2>

            <p id="gato-class"><strong>Gato class</strong> &ndash; The same size as the USS Pampanito but with a thinner hull. The design depth was 450 feet, but they were only tested to 300 to provide a 50% margin of error. 300 feet was the &ldquo;test depth.&rdquo; The first Gato boats were ordered and construction started in 1940. These initial boats were completed and commissioned in November and December of 1941 at about the time of the attack on Pearl Harbor.</p>

            <p id="balao-class"><strong>Balao class</strong> &ndash; USS Pampanito is a Balao class boat. Design depth is 600 feet; test depth is around 400 feet. The first Balao boat was completed in February of 1943.</p>

            <p id="tench-class"><strong>Tench class</strong> &ndash; These are very much like the Balao boats but with some piping changes for added safety. The first one was completed in October of 1944.</p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4 mb-3">After WW2</h2>

            <p>After WW2, we built a limited number of diesel-electric boats, again incorporating what we learned from captured U-boats. We also added new features, such as snorkels and improved batteries. The most significant change was, of course, the switch to nuclear power. With the Nautilus in 1954, we were building true submarines. These were vessels intended to operate in the depths most of the time. They were not just submersibles like their predecessors. Again, we built a few small classes of boats to prove concepts. By the early 1960s, we were building larger classes of nuclear-powered &ldquo;attack&rdquo; submarines along with submarines that carried ICBMs &mdash; Polaris missiles with nuclear warheads.</p>

            <p>In the early 1970s, we began to name our nuclear-powered attack submarines after cities. However, a few were named after people who were significant to our submarine service, such as President Jimmy Carter and ADM Hyman Rickover. Now many are named after states. Some of the attack submarines now being planned will be named after historic submarines. Others will be named after cities once again.</p>

            <p>Missile boats built in the early 1960s &mdash; our 41 Polaris submarines &mdash; were named after patriots. These boats have all been decommissioned. The Trident missile boats currently in use are named after states. The replacement missile submarines now being built will also be named after states, plus the District of Columbia.</p>

            <p>We are no longer as consistent with our submarine names as we used to be.</p>
